WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.140
Today's topic is one of the most famous stories in statistics,

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:11.040
uh, it's also about where babies come from. Um, and I know you know where babies come from, but there used to be this tale,

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.160
that's what I grew up with, that babies come from storks.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:21.199
If a child is asking awkward questions of his or her parents, the parents would say well, you know, the stalks bring the baby

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.640
in - draped from their bill in a blanket,

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.439
that's where your baby brother or your baby sister came from.
- (Brady: Have you figured out where that came from?)

00:00:29.519 --> 00:00:31.599
(Why storks were associated with babies at all?)

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:41.200
So there's a theory it's something to do with um stalks being a traditional symbol of fertility, a religious symbol of fertility. And another theory

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.600
is that storks when they go fishing they're looking for embryonic life forms in the water,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.160
so that is, that is two theories. Um,

00:00:50.719 --> 00:00:53.519
I don't know. I'm, I'm an economist, you know, I'm not a,

00:00:54.079 --> 00:01:00.959
not a sort of a studier of mythology and ritual. Anyway, anyway, to get back to the important stuff, which is the statistics. So

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:05.039
storks deliver babies. This is the old story.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.760
Let me try to prove that to you statistically. I've got my, I've got my paper here.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.040
So,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.859
and this is just going to be a um illustrative plotting, if you imagine two axes.

00:01:16.479 --> 00:01:18.959
And here, this is babies per year,

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:20.479
and here

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:26.479
we have the number of storks. And if you just take a a bunch of European countries, and I have actually seen the data,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.619
what you find is

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:35.119
Turkey, if I remember, and this is Poland. And then you might have Germany here and then you've got a whole bunch down here, a

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:40.899
couple there. And it's not the world's best correlation, but it's, it's not the world's worst correlation.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.959
Loads down here with not many babies, not many storks. You've got these big outliers here. You've got nothing here. You've got no places

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.399
with um loads of babies and no storks,

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.680
you've got no places with loads of storks and no babies, and you can draw a best fit line and it goes like that,

00:01:55.759 --> 00:02:02.718
and there is actually an academic paper, about 20 years ago, with the title storks deliver babies p equals

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:10.959
0.008. And without going into the technical details, you probably know that according to the conventional standards of statistical significance

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:14.958
that means this relationship is not a coincidence. There is definitely a

00:02:15.819 --> 00:02:19.598
correlation between storks and babies. Now, you're probably thinking to yourself, okay

00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:23.759
so what's the trick? Because surprise surprise storks don't actually deliver babies.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.679
Um, email me if you actually want me to explain where babies do come from.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.400
(Brady: Will they get a reply?)
[both laugh]

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.800
Well, maybe.

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:38.959
So what's going on here? What's going on here is basically, as I said, I think this is Turkey, this is Poland.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:45.120
There's, there's Monaco, there's Luxembourg, there's Lichtenstein down here. Lots and lots of places with very small amounts of land,

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.839
there are no storks and there are no kids, basically. And here are places that are really big, lots of storks,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.239
lots of babies. And that's what's going on, that's enough

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.839
to drive that statistical correlation. The amount of land is the hidden, uh is the hidden factor,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.479
yeah,
it's the secret thing that's driving both a large population, which means a lot of babies,

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:08.079
and it is driving the large number of storks. Because bear in mind I'm not - I didn't say birth rate,

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:12.158
I'm sure there's, well I expect there's no correlation for the birth rate,

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.639
what i'm talking about is the the number of babies born each year in this,

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:23.759
in these countries. Which is very closely correlated to the population. 
- (Brady: So you would also perhaps expect a correlation with trees?)

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.280
Yeah, yeah, probably number of trees versus number of babies a year? Trees also deliver babies,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.119
I mean you could, you could go in all kinds of places like this. And there are lots of these,

00:03:33.519 --> 00:03:39.279
um, these spurious correlations out there. Actually, there's a website maintained by a guy called Tyler Vigen,

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.039
I think, called spurious correlations; full of all kinds of fun stuff like this.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.319
But I said at the beginning that this is one of the most famous stories in statistics.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.720
And it was made famous by a fellow called Darrell Huff and,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.500
here for me is where this gets really interesting. So Darrell Huff was a journalist. And in 1954

00:03:59.519 --> 00:04:00.959
he published a book

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:06.079
with the title "How to lie with statistics". It got a rave review in the New York Times,

00:04:06.239 --> 00:04:13.839
it became, many people say, the best selling book about statistics ever written. And I loved it when I was a teenager,

00:04:14.799 --> 00:04:20.559
reading about all of these tricks, all of the different ways you could be deceived. He's got the storks and babies in there,

00:04:20.639 --> 00:04:25.599
it's got loads and loads of great examples. It's got dodgy maps, it's got dodgy graphs, it's got

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.679
all sorts of great, great examples. It's a very funny book.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.320
Um, I'm not the only person who likes it, Ben Goldacre, who wrote bad science and many other great books about numbers,

00:04:36.479 --> 00:04:43.279
he praised Darrell Huff. Charles Wheelan, who wrote a book called naked statistics; he said it was an homage to Darrell Huff.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.639
There are loads of fans of Darrell Huff out there. 
- (Brady: This book is in praise of statistics, though.)

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.919
(This, this book is supposed to be doing a service to statistics, presumably?)
- So that, I think, is where

00:04:54.639 --> 00:04:56.639
the important question, uh lies.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.520
It, it is loved by nerds,

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:03.919
but it is not really in praise of statistics because what Darrell Huff is doing

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.660
is in chapter after chapter; example after example, he's debunking

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:16.959
dodgy statistics. And he just doesn't have a lot of positive examples of statistics being used to illuminate the world.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.120
And that's where I

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:26.239
start to get a little bit queasy. Because that book was published in 1954, and you know, what else happened in 1954? Two great statisticians,

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:33.460
epidemiologists, Richard Dole and Austin Bradford Hill, provided some of the first solid evidence that smoking cigarettes

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:35.919
might well give you lung cancer.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:43.039
Because the same year, two different visions of what you can do with statistics. Darrell Huff is saying it's a bit of a joke,

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:49.199
it's a bit of fun - I'll tell you the story about the storks and the babies. Um, but don't don't ever take statistics, too

00:05:49.199 --> 00:05:51.839
seriously because they're just a way for people to lie to you.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.880
Austin Bradford Hill and Richard Dole

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:00.639
are using this vital tool to find out the answer to one of the key

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.720
medical questions of the 20th century. And when you think about it,

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:08.639
they couldn't have done that in any other way than using a, the statistical lens.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.759
So even to notice that lung cancer cases were on the increase, and they were massively,

00:06:14.319 --> 00:06:15.500
you need to be

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.759
systematically counting cases of lung cancer.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:24.959
I mean you, maybe there'd be some anecdotes, but you know, you need the numbers, you need the data. And then to show, compellingly,

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:28.339
that uh, there's, there's evidence of causation

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:31.039
you really need to work hard with the statistics.

00:06:31.199 --> 00:06:35.119
A lot of people at the time thought it was something to do with cars, which is not crazy,

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:38.979
you think about it, between the beginning of the 20th century and the 1950s,

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.319
lung cancer's on the increase, so are cars - loads of cars. Maybe it's something in the tarmac?

00:06:44.479 --> 00:06:47.839
Maybe it's uh something, you know coming out of the exhaust? It's not a crazy idea.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:50.560
But it turns out it wasn't the cars,

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:52.560
it was the cigarettes.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:57.440
This is so important. This is such a key fact. Understanding that this thing is true,

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.799
this has saved literally tens of millions of lives. Possibly hundreds of millions of lives.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:07.759
And you've got Darrell Huff in, remember this the the best-selling book ever

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.400
published about statistics, saying

00:07:11.039 --> 00:07:16.000
'don't ever take statistics too seriously' because they're basically,

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.000
liars will use them to lie to you. There's a twist in this story. Because in the

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:24.318
1960s, the US Senate held hearings to decide

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:26.720
whether cigarette

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:30.559
packets should have warning labels on them. Should smokers be warned, you know,

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:37.119
maybe this is going to kill you. And they got all these expert witnesses in to discuss the evidence and one expert witness came in

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.280
and he told the senators:

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:41.039
'Look, don't worry too much

00:07:41.039 --> 00:07:42.400
because

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:44.400
there are a lot of funny correlations out there.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:47.840
Let me tell you about storks and babies.'

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:52.979
And he sat there and he told the senators all about how you could demonstrate this correlation

00:07:53.599 --> 00:07:54.639
between

00:07:54.639 --> 00:07:57.839
babies and storks, he used a slightly different example, but the same basic argument.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.199
And it's the same with, with cigarettes and lung cancer. And the senator who was chairing the commission said:

00:08:03.919 --> 00:08:05.919
'Do you seriously mean to tell me

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:07.599
that

00:08:07.599 --> 00:08:11.839
there is as as casual a relationship between smoking and lung cancer

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:14.959
as there is between storks and babies?'

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:21.359
And the expert witness says 'yeah, they seem to me to be about the same.' And his name,

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.759
you may have seen it coming,

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:26.479
Darrell Huff, the guy who published

00:08:27.199 --> 00:08:34.658
'How to lie with statistics'. He was actually working on a sequel, which was never published, called how to lie with smoking statistics.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:36.719
So he'd taken

00:08:36.719 --> 00:08:39.199
this, you know, this, you know, funny, witty

00:08:39.899 --> 00:08:41.120
skepticism

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:47.440
about statistics, in this great little book, and he'd soured it into this nasty cynicism

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.080
that everything out there is fake news. You can't believe a single thing you're told,

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:56.580
and he'd then gone to work for the tobacco lobby. Who found him to be the perfect

00:08:57.279 --> 00:08:59.279
witness for their case.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:05.919
If you'd like more great stories like this from Tim Harford, check out his book "How to Make the World Add Up".

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.880
This is what it looks like, just in case you need to know.

